      "resolved": false,
      "bot": false,
      "body": "⚠️ severity: IMPORTANT —
⚠️ **IMPORTANT: The hermeticity guard is tautological — it can never red, so the gh-2192 regression it targets would land green**

`realMeasurements` is declared at `ContendedClock` (`var realMeasurements = 0;`, line 156) and asserted here (line 703), but **no code path ever writes it** — the new `call()` implementation never touches it:

```dart
int call(void Function() body) {
  calls++;
  final doubled = ((calls - 1) % 10).isOdd; // s,d,s,d,… per attempt
  final base = doubled ? doubledUs : smallUs;
  return base + (_spiked.contains(calls) ? (spikeMs * 1000).round() : 0);
}
```

`expect(clock.realMeasurements, 0)` therefore asserts a constant. Worse, the exact regression class this guard exists to catch survives it: reverting `call()` to the pre-PR stopwatch form (execute `body()`, return real elapsed + spike — the gh-2192 shape) leaves `realMeasurements == 0`, `calls == 30`, and every ratio scripted at 2.0, so **all assertions here stay green while the modeling scenario is again CI-weather-dependent**. The ticket's Fix bullet ("New hermeticity guard reds if a modeling scenario ever consults the real clock again" — `input/pr-2193/ticket.md:13`) and this file's own guard comment ("This guard reds the moment any measurement … consults real elapsed time again", line 694) promise a red this code cannot produce. Note the ticket's RED evidence ("guard failed against the old harness (30 real-clock measurements)") is only reproducible against a counter-instrumented variant of the old clock — the shipped code has no such wiring, so a plain revert defeats the guard.

Suggested fix — make the probe structural instead of convention-based. A scripted clock must never *execute* the body (executing it is how real timing was done), so assert that directly in this guard:

```dart
final probe = ContendedClock(spiked: {});
var bodyRan = false;
probe.call(() => bodyRan = true);
expect(bodyRan, isFalse,
    reason: 'a scripted clock executed its body — that means real work '
        'is being timed again (the gh-2192 mechanism)');
```

Any stopwatch-based revert executes the body and reds deterministically. Then either wire `realMeasurements++` into every real-timing path in `call()` (so the field means something) or delete the dead field — today it reads as a working tripwire but is a constant."
    },
    {
      "index": 2,
